irq - interrupt lines held outside the kernel

Each line a service holds, whether it is armed, how it is triggered, how
many services wait on it, and how many times it has delivered. The first
question when a device has gone quiet.

A line delivering nothing is hardware that is not reaching this system
at all, which is a different fault from one that delivers and is not
understood: a driver counting no packets on a line that fires is being
spoken to and failing to listen. Lines are shared on this machine's
wiring, so a line with several owners wakes all of them and each reads
its own device to find out whether it was the one that spoke.

`forced` appears only when it is news: a completion the kernel had to
make itself names a driver that stopped answering for its line.
